  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nLatest GOES Water Vapor imagery shows an upper level low\ndeparting the area to the east. Rain showers continue to rotate \nacross parts of the area but these will gradually diminish by \ndaybreak. This low did bring a much needed, soaking rainfall to \nmost areas along and north of I-90 with amounts generally \nbetween 0.50 and 1.50 inches. Less rain occurred to the south. \nMoist, cyclonic flow behind the departing low will lead to some \nadditional light showers for west to northwesterly-favored \nupslope areas including the Adirondacks, Mohawk Valley, eastern \nCatskills, Taconics and into western New England into the \nmorning hours before brief upper level ridging brings and end to\nprecipitation this afternoon with clouds breaking for a few \nhours of sunshine. Winds will pick up with occasional gusts of \n20-30 mph from late this morning through the afternoon. High \ntemperatures will be right near seasonal values in the mid-40s \nto upper 50s.\n\nA series of disturbances will bring additional opportunities for\nlight rainfall through mid-week. The first disturbance arrives\ntonight and continues through Monday and will bring the \ngreatest coverage of showers. The Tuesday and Wednesday \ndisturbances will favor showers for areas mainly north of I-90.\nBehind the Monday disturbance, it will become breezy once again\nwith gusts 20 to 30 mph. Precipitation will not be heavy enough\nwith any of these to cause any flooding concerns. Temperatures \nMonday through Wednesday will run slightly above normal for this\ntime of year.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nConfidence continues to increase in a large-scale trough to\ndevelop over the eastern CONUS later this week into next\nweekend. This will favor a trend toward below normal\ntemperatures. Whether or not we are impacted by a storm system\nduring this time remains highly uncertain. By later this week, a\nlow pressure system will likely approach the region from the\nOhio Valley. The uncertainty increases whether or not this low\ntransfers its energy to a coastal low (Miller-B type system) or\nremains the dominant feature. In addition, if the coastal low\nforms, uncertainty exists whether it tracks close enough to \nbring widespread precipitation to the region or farther away to\nbring just a glancing blow. The latest global ensemble means \nand AI forecasts suggest a coastal low will form but be more \nsuppressed to the south and track far enough off the coast (less\nphasing) to bring minimal precipitation and impacts to the \nregion. Even with this solution, a mix of rain and snow will be \npossible but accumulating snowfall potential will be reduced \nwith the lack of efficient dynamic cooling. However, there \nremain some members that bring a system closer to the coast to \nbring greater precipitation and impacts but that solution looks \nto be the outlier at this time. This system is still several \ndays out and model outcomes will likely continue to fluctuate in\nthe coming days until they later converge on a more likely \nsolution.",
